{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1463463-new-frailties-tells-triggers","id":1463463,"title":"New Frailties: Tells & Triggers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-10-11T13:59:01-07:00","id":1463463,"title":"New Frailties: Tells & Triggers","content":"The myriad mythic weaknesses, banes, quirks, and idiosyncrasies of the fae could fill tome after tome. Folklore paints their habits and taboos with a palette of a thousand-and-one shades, and what's good for the goose may not in fact be good for the gander by any stretch of the imagination. Faerie is a place of one-offs, unique phenomena, maybes, and exceptions to every rule; changelings often bring that with them back to the waking world, whether they want to or not.<br /> <br /> Although Taboos and Banes are the most common frailties the Lost develop as they attune themselves further and further to the Wyrd, other weaknesses on occasion manifest. Two new categories are presented here:<br /> <br /> <b>Tells</b><br /> <br /> The Mask is a powerful disguise, but it is not a perfect one. Some changelings who study the illusion and with a propensity for anthropomorphism (not difficult or unwise when dealing with Arcadia) describe it as being coy, as though it wants to reveal the fae for what they really are, but only through play and whim. Maybe so. As changelings grow more powerful and accumulate more Glamour, the Mask sometimes begins to falter, letting bits and pieces of the Mien slip through. At best these are minor slips: an eye whose pupil looks vaguely reptilian, skin that is for a brief second freezing to the touch. At worst, the cracks are catastrophic, greatly hindering the changeling's ability to interact with mortals or hide from Huntsmen.<br /> <br /> <i>Systems:</i> A minor tell is a small aspect of the changeling's true nature slipping through the Mask. It's the sort of thing that could be explained away as cosmetics, a genetic quirk, an old injury, or the like. The Flowering always smells like blooming jasmine, the Beast has coarse hair growing on the palms of his hands, the Gristlegrinder's teeth look unusually sharp, like she's got a mouth full of canines. These tells can be disguised with an appropriate roll (such as Wits + Subterfuge), and usually require a successful Perception roll on the part of mortals to notice them in the first place; if the changeling successfully disguised his tell, the mortal must match or exceed the Lost's own successes.<br /> <br /> A major tell is much more difficult to hide: Flammable materials begin to smolder if the Elemental stays in one place for too long, the Manikin has a voice like a text-to-speech reader, the skin all over the Wizened's body is gray like one of those Roswell aliens. Rolls to disguise these tells (if possible at all) suffer a -3 penalty, and mortals do not have to make a Perception roll to notice their presence.<br /> <br /> Either version of a tell might inflict the Notoriety Condition on the Lost if enough (or the right) people become aware of it, as word spreads and the changeling finds herself increasingly ostracized or under heavier scrutiny. Freeholds, courts, and motleys provide safe structures for changelings who can't safely venture out among humans, but such guardianship can begin to chafe after a while. Maybe a life in the Hedge wouldn't be so bad after all? Tells can be suppressed temporarily by strengthening the Mask, though any physical properties are still visible in the changeling's shadow.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Triggers</b><br /> <br /> Arcadia is a land of wonder, terror, beauty, awe, salvation, damnation, dreams, whimsy, truth, lies, and everything in between. Arcadia makes no fucking sense. Neither does the mortal world either, frankly. All these rules: the social contract, gravity, weather, taxes, geometry, human rights. As a changeling becomes increasingly ensconced in a web of oaths and bargains, things that violate their sense of their place in existence become ever more distressful. For most Lost, this is part of a natural process of attempting to balance both their fae and human halves, but some find the Wyrd ladening them with even greater mental burdens. Maybe their time among the Gentry was especially traumatic, or maybe the Wyrd is making good on a promise they broke long ago. Or maybe there's no rhyme or reason to any of it, the disordered thoughts from barking dogs and horror at the sight of the Pepsico label meaning nothing greater than another manifestation of the pulsing chaos at the heart of all things.<br /> <br /> <i>Systems:</i> Triggers are additional causes for Clarity attacks imposed by the Wyrd. A minor trigger is something not particularly common, that the changeling might have to go out of their way to experience, like the taste of a particular herb, or hearing that awful, grating severe weather warning siren. Major triggers are much more common, such as the sight of fish, getting caught in the rain, or eating bread. A changeling with a major trigger will have to carefully consider how to structure their life so as not to spiral every time they leave the Hollow. Both types of triggers use a Clarity attack dice pool of half the changeling's Wyrd rating, rounded up; a major trigger scores an exceptional success on such a roll with only three successes.<br /> <br /> Since only changelings possess Clarity, triggers normally aren't appropriate as frailties for other types of fae creatures, though if the Storyteller wishes to give one to a hobgoblin or the like, the reaction can be represented with a Condition such as Frightened or Spooked.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1423,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","user":"espritdecalmar","date":". 10-11-2021, 05:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-13T12:10:37-07:00","id":1463735,"title":"","content":"As far as triggers go, the main thing to note is triggers in the mundane psychological sense of the term should already cause clarity bp. It might be better as something similar to seeming curses - something that wouldn't conceivably be a trigger for a changeling for their background.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25382-tzauri","name":"Tzauri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}